# Chapter 9 · The Schools Compared — and Which Feminism Suits Pakistan?

> This chapter brings the schools together. It gives you the master comparison table, the three comparisons the examiner likes most (radical versus socialist, liberal versus radical, Marxist versus socialist), and a reasoned answer to the 2018 question: "What type(s) of feminism can elevate the socio-cultural, economic and political status of women in Pakistan?"

## 9.1  The master table

::: table | All the schools compared on one page.
@widths 2.3,2.8,2.6,3,3.3
School | Cause of oppression | Remedy | Key thinkers (dates) | Main weakness
Liberal | Unequal laws and opportunities | Reform: rights, education, laws | Wollstonecraft 1792; Mill 1869; Friedan 1963 | Ignores structures and the family
Radical | Patriarchy, the root | Transform culture and family; bodily autonomy | Millett 1970; Firestone 1970; Hanisch 1970 | Treats all women as one; essentialism
Marxist | Capitalism and private property | End capitalism; socialise housework | Engels 1884; Zetkin 1910; Benston 1969 | "Sex-blind"; reduces gender to class
Socialist | Capitalism **and** patriarchy | Change economy and family together | Mitchell 1971; Hartmann 1979; Young 1980 | Hard to say how the two systems link
Psychoanalytic | The unconscious; early childhood | Shared parenting; new meanings | Horney 1926; Mitchell 1974; Chodorow 1978 | Untestable; ignores material life
Men's | Patriarchy shapes and harms men too | Men as allies; change masculinity | Mill 1869; Connell 1995; hooks 2004 | Can centre men
Postmodern | Fixed categories; discourse | Deconstruct; value difference | Cixous 1975; Butler 1990; Flax 1987 | Relativism; loses "women" as a category
Intersectional | Overlapping systems | Policies for specific groups | Crenshaw 1989; Collins 1990 | Can fragment into endless categories
Postcolonial | Colonial and Western dominance | Local, self-defined feminisms | Mohanty 1984; Spivak 1988 | Can be used to defend harmful custom
Ecofeminist | Domination of women and nature | Sustainable, care-based economy | d'Eaubonne 1974; Shiva 1988 | Essentialism (women "closer" to nature)
Muslim | Patriarchal interpretation of Islam | Re-read sources; reform family law | Mernissi 1987; Wadud 1992; Barlas 2002 | Criticised by both secularists and conservatives
:::

::: fig matrix | The schools placed by how much change they want (across) and where they locate the problem (up and down).
@x Reform  →  Transformation
@y Structures above; mind and language below
Reform the structures: Liberal and men's feminism change laws, institutions and the men who run them
Transform the structures: Radical feminism uproots patriarchy; Marxist and socialist feminism change the economy and family
Change the inner life: Psychoanalytic feminism changes childrearing and the unconscious
Unsettle meanings: Postmodern feminism deconstructs language, categories and identity
:::

## 9.2  Radical versus socialist feminism (2016)

This is the comparison the examiner asked for directly. Both are "second-wave" schools of the late 1960s and 1970s, and both want **deep change** rather than reform. They differ on the **root cause**.

::: fig compare | Radical feminism and socialist feminism.
## Radical feminism
- Root: patriarchy — older than capitalism
- Women are a "sex class"
- Site: family, sexuality, body, violence
- Men as a group benefit from oppression
- Method: consciousness-raising, women-only spaces
- Thinkers: Millett, Firestone, Hanisch
## Socialist feminism
- Root: capitalism and patriarchy together
- Women are divided by class as well as united by sex
- Site: paid work, housework and family
- Capitalism and male privilege are linked
- Method: alliances with workers' movements
- Thinkers: Mitchell, Hartmann, Eisenstein
:::

::: table | Radical and socialist feminism point by point.
@widths 3,5.5,5.5
Point | Radical feminism | Socialist feminism
Main enemy | **Patriarchy** | **Capitalist patriarchy**
Unit of analysis | **Sex** (women as a class) | **Sex and class** together
Would ending capitalism free women? | **No** — patriarchy came first and survives in every economy | **Not alone** — both systems must be changed
View of men | Men as a group are the beneficiaries | Working-class men are also exploited; they can be allies
Key concepts | Sexual politics, sex class, personal is political | Dual systems, family wage, double burden, social reproduction
Typical campaign | Rape crisis centres, shelters, bodily autonomy | Equal pay, childcare, wages for housework, rights of women workers
Main criticism | Ignores class and race; "anti-men" image | Too economic; neglects violence and sexuality
:::

::: remember
The one-line difference: **radical feminism** sees **patriarchy alone** as the root; **socialist feminism** sees **patriarchy and capitalism** working together. They **agree** that the family is a site of oppression and that "the personal is political".
:::

## 9.3  Two more comparisons

::: table | Liberal versus radical feminism.
@widths 3,5.5,5.5
Point | Liberal | Radical
Cause | Unequal laws and opportunities | Patriarchy
Strategy | **Reform** within the system | **Transform** the system
Sphere | Public — law, work, politics | Private and public — family, body, sexuality
Equality means | Same rights and chances as men | Ending male power in every area
Example law | Equal Pay Act 1963; Harassment Act 2010 (Pakistan) | Laws on rape and domestic violence; shelters
:::

::: table | Marxist versus socialist feminism (summary; full detail in Chapter 4).
@widths 3,5.5,5.5
Point | Marxist | Socialist
Enemy | Capitalism | Capitalism **and** patriarchy
Gender is | A form of class oppression | Equal in importance to class
Key thinker | Engels (1884) | Hartmann (1979)
:::

## 9.4  Which feminism suits Pakistan? (2018)

### The question and the trap

The question says "type(s)" — the examiner invites you to argue for **more than one**. The trap is to pick one school and reject the rest, or to write a history of the Pakistani women's movement (that belongs to Topic 3). Instead, match each school to a **dimension of status** named in the question: **socio-cultural, economic and political**.

### The Pakistani context in five facts

::: data Women's status in Pakistan | latest figures
- Global Gender Gap Report ==2026==: Pakistan ==143rd of 145==, with ==59.5%== of the gap closed (economic participation ==144th==, education ==130th==, political empowerment ==107th==).
- Female labour-force participation: ==24.4%== (Labour Force Survey 2024–25).
- Literacy: about ==68%== men and ==53%== women (Census 2023).
- Maternal mortality ratio: ==186== per 100,000 live births (Pakistan Maternal Mortality Survey 2019).
- Only ==11== women won general seats in the National Assembly in ==2024==; women's presence rests mainly on the ==60== reserved seats.
:::

### Matching schools to dimensions

::: fig mapping | Which school addresses which dimension of women's status in Pakistan.
@left The school
@right The dimension of status
Liberal feminism: rights, laws, education => Political and legal status
Men's feminism: engaging fathers and leaders => Political and legal status
Islamic feminism: rights from within the faith => Socio-cultural status
Postcolonial feminism: local, self-defined => Socio-cultural status
Radical insights: violence and bodily safety => Socio-cultural status
Socialist feminism: work, pay, unpaid care => Economic status
Intersectionality: which women? => All three
:::

1. **Liberal feminism — the legal and political floor.** It is anchored in **Articles 25, 27 and 34** of the Constitution. It gives quotas, anti-harassment and anti-violence laws, and girls' education. Its weakness is enforcement.
2. **Islamic (Muslim) feminism — cultural legitimacy.** In a society where most people see life through faith, rights argued from the Qur'an — **inheritance**, ***mahr***, ***khula***, **consent**, **education** — meet less resistance. The Federal Shariat Court and superior courts have repeatedly affirmed such rights.
3. **Socialist feminism — economic status.** Women's low labour participation, unpaid farm and care work, home-based piece work and low wages need economic answers: **childcare, safe transport, recognition of unpaid work, labour rights for home-based and domestic workers, and cash transfers** such as the Benazir Income Support Programme.
4. **Radical insights — safety and bodily integrity.** The work against **honour killing, acid attacks, rape and domestic violence** comes from the radical insight that violence controls women. Pakistan's laws of 2011, 2016 and 2021 reflect it.
5. **Men's feminism — the gatekeepers.** Fathers, husbands, brothers, clerics and police must be engaged, because they control most decisions about women's lives.
6. **Intersectionality — the targeting tool.** It ensures that policies reach **rural, poor, minority and disabled women** and *khawaja siras*, not only the urban middle class.

::: fig pyramid | A layered feminism for Pakistan: from the foundation up.
Foundation: constitutional equality (liberal) — Articles 25, 27, 34
Legitimacy: rights from within the faith (Islamic feminism)
Livelihood: work, pay and care (socialist feminism)
Safety: protection from violence (radical insight)
Reach: rural, poor and minority women first (intersectionality)
:::

### Which schools fit least — and why they still help

- **Pure radical feminism**, with separatism and slogans on sexuality, provokes backlash and is easy to paint as foreign. Its analysis of violence is useful; its style is not.
- **Pure Marxist feminism** waits for a revolution that is not coming; its insights on unpaid work are useful through socialist and welfare policies.
- **Postmodern feminism** is too abstract for policy, but it warns against speaking of one "Pakistani woman".
- **Psychoanalytic feminism** gives no quick policy, but its message about **shared parenting** and raising boys differently is valuable for long-term change.

::: balance | The conclusion to write
No single imported school can raise women's status in Pakistan. What works is a **plural, home-grown feminism**: **liberal** in law, **Islamic** in cultural justification, **socialist** in economic policy, **radical** in its refusal to tolerate violence, **inclusive of men**, and **intersectional** in whom it reaches first. This is not a compromise of principles; it is the way Pakistan's own most successful reforms — from the Muslim Family Laws Ordinance of ==1961== to the honour-killing law of ==2016== — were actually won.
:::

::: eye | The model structure for 2018
1. Introduction: status in three dimensions; Pakistan's gap in figures. 2. What feminism is; why one school is not enough. 3–8. Liberal, Islamic, socialist, radical insight, men's feminism and intersectionality — each with what it offers, a Pakistani law or programme, and its limit. 9. Schools that fit less and what they still offer. 10. The layered model (pyramid figure). 11. Conclusion: a plural, home-grown feminism. A mapping figure of school → dimension earns extra marks.
:::
